// A real connection, end to end, across the listener this increment added:
// `bind` on port 0, `local_port` to learn the kernel's choice, `accept`
// parking until a client arrives, then one round trip over the accepted
// stream. Unlike every other `std/net` fixture, this one needs no Rust peer
// in `run_tests.rs` -- both ends are Nova, in two tasks of one process.
//
// **This is `local_port`'s only runtime fixture.** Until it existed, nothing
// under `tests/runtime` called `local_port` at all, and that gap was not
// merely a missing test: `RtFunc::NetClose` and `RtFunc::NetLocalPort` are
// *indistinguishable* in `RtFunc::signature` (`crates/nova-mir/src/lib.rs`)
// -- both `(vec![I64], I64)` -- so a lowering arm naming one where it meant
// the other emits a call neither that table nor LLVM's
// `every_rt_func_is_declared_with_its_real_signature` can fault. The
// `NetClose`-as-`NetLocalPort` direction was already caught by
// `net_lifetime.stdout`'s `close: ok`; the reverse direction was caught by
// nothing. It is caught here, and that was **measured rather than reasoned
// about**: with `local_port`'s body pointed at `net_close`, the first line
// below flips to `server: port in range false` and the second becomes
// `nova: panic: accept: socket is not open`, exit 127 -- so the swap fails
// both this golden and the test's `.success()`, on the *first* line, with no
// hang anywhere. (Beware measuring this the cheap way: `std` is embedded in
// the compiler by `include_str!`, so editing `std/net/lib.nova` and rerunning
// a stale `nova` binary reports a false pass. It must be rebuilt.)
//
// **The port is asserted by value, not merely by `Ok`.** Two ways, because
// neither alone is enough, and the first is load-bearing precisely because of
// what the measurement above found. The range check below is weak (it rules
// out 0 and a decode that overflowed, nothing finer) but it is *deterministic
// and first*, so a bad port is a named golden mismatch rather than a client
// dialling into the void. The second is that the client connects to *that
// reported number* and the server's `accept` returns, which no wrong port can
// fake. The exact number is deliberately absent from the golden -- the kernel
// chooses it, so printing it would make the golden unmatchable.
//
// **PORT 0 IS REQUIRED, NOT PREFERRED.** A fixed port collides whenever two
// test binaries run concurrently, and this suite runs its fixtures in
// parallel. Note the *other* half of that: this fixture holds its listener
// from `bind` to the `close` on the last line, so it cannot reproduce the
// bind-then-drop shape `write_refused_port_file` in
// `crates/nova-cli/tests/run_tests.rs` documents as an accepted race -- there
// is no window here in which the kernel could hand this port to anyone else.
//
// **NO CHANNEL, deliberately.** `recv` on an empty channel spins
// unconditionally (`channel_recv_suspends_only_to_retry.nova`), so a consumer
// waiting on a socket-parked producer could never be woken. None is needed:
// `bind` and `local_port` are plain `fn`s that cannot suspend, so the port is
// known before anything parks and travels to the client as a plain `spawn`
// argument.
//
// **ONE SOCKET WAIT PER TASK, and that is why there are two tasks.** Staging
// two I/O waits in a single poll ends the process (`stage_park` in
// `crates/nova-runtime/src/task.rs`), so the task parked in `accept` cannot
// also be the task reading a connection, and this language has no `select` to
// fold them. Sequential waits within one task are fine -- `main` below does
// `accept`, then `read`, then `write` -- and it is only two staged in a single
// poll that aborts.
//
// **NO ASSERTION ON ELAPSED TIME**, and none is needed, because the output
// order here is forced by data dependency rather than by scheduling. The
// client's one line cannot print until its `read` returns, which cannot
// happen until the server has echoed, which cannot happen until the server's
// own two lines have printed. So whichever of the two parked tasks the poller
// happens to wake first -- and either order is legitimate, since a loopback
// `connect` and the listener's readiness settle together -- the six lines
// below come out in exactly one order. `main`'s last line follows a `join`,
// so it is last. That is what makes this fixture safe to run in parallel with
// the rest of the suite; it is not a timing assertion at all.
//
// What this does *not* pin: that `accept` genuinely parks rather than
// blocking the executor thread. Nothing in this output can tell those apart,
// for exactly the reason `net_roundtrip.nova`'s header gives about `connect`
// -- both eventually produce the same bytes. The park is pinned on the
// runtime side instead, by
// `accept_parks_until_a_client_connects_then_yields_a_stream_fd` in
// `crates/nova-runtime/src/net.rs`.
//
// **THREE OF THE SIX GOLDEN LINES REST ON CIRCUMSTANCE, NOT ON CONTRACT**, and
// a reader changing the payload needs to know which. `Write::write` here is
// one non-blocking attempt and **may legally write fewer bytes than given**
// (`std/net/lib.nova`'s own `write_stream` comment says so); `read` is
// equally permissive, since a short read is explicitly *not* end of stream.
// So `server: read 18 bytes`, `server: echoed 18 bytes` and -- through the
// client's own `read(64)` -- `client: echo matches true` all hold because an
// 18-byte payload crosses loopback in a single segment, not because anything
// guarantees it. They are reliable at this size and would stay reliable well
// past it; they are not contractual at any size.
//
// The consequence is bounded, which is why this is a warning and not a
// redesign: a short read or write makes a line *differ* from the golden, so
// the fixture fails loudly rather than passing on a weaker property. If you
// raise the payload past one segment, expect these three lines to need
// either a loop or a different assertion -- and do not read a failure there
// as a regression in `accept`. The other three lines are contractual:
// `server: port in range true` is pinned by the mutation described in
// `RtFunc::signature`'s comment, and both `closed` lines by
// `TcpListener::close`'s documented kind-agnostic table removal.
async fn client(port: Int) {
    let payload = "hi from the client"
    let s = match connect("127.0.0.1:${port}").await {
        Ok(s) => s
        Err(e) => panic("client connect: ${e.message}")
    }
    let _ = match s.write(bytes_from_string(payload)).await {
        Ok(count) => count
        Err(e) => panic("client write: ${e.message}")
    }
    let echoed = match s.read(64).await {
        Ok(b) => b
        Err(e) => panic("client read: ${e.message}")
    }
    match echoed.to_string() {
        Some(text) => println("client: echo matches ${text == payload}")
        None => println("client: echo <non-utf8>")
    }
    let _ = s.close().await
}

async fn main() {
    let l = match bind("127.0.0.1:0") {
        Ok(l) => l
        Err(e) => panic("bind: ${e.message}")
    }
    let port = match l.local_port() {
        Ok(p) => p
        Err(e) => panic("local_port: ${e.message}")
    }
    println("server: port in range ${(port > 0) && (port < 65536)}")

    let h = spawn(client(port))

    let conn = match l.accept().await {
        Ok(c) => c
        Err(e) => panic("accept: ${e.message}")
    }
    let got = match conn.read(64).await {
        Ok(b) => b
        Err(e) => panic("server read: ${e.message}")
    }
    println("server: read ${got.len()} bytes")
    let sent = match conn.write(got).await {
        Ok(count) => count
        Err(e) => panic("server write: ${e.message}")
    }
    println("server: echoed ${sent} bytes")
    match conn.close().await {
        Ok(_) => println("server: conn closed")
        Err(e) => panic("server conn close: ${e.message}")
    }

    h.join().await

    match l.close().await {
        Ok(_) => println("server: listener closed")
        Err(e) => panic("listener close: ${e.message}")
    }
}
